>h2 CupidBuild Object Transaction
>tree open CupidBuild Object Transaction
The source-head CupidBuild command can assemble a private CupidASM ET_REL
object after it parses the frozen five-tool seed contract. CupidDis requires
complete known decoding, local targets, and function anchors at instruction
starts before publication. The Linux and Windows hosted layers reject links, hardlink
aliases, drift, competing publishers, and changed output parents. A replaced
owner lock found at a final boundary blocks publication. Cleanup quarantines
the path and restores a successor instead of deleting it. Every failure
preserves the previous object. The command remains a source-head proof until a
checked seed contains CupidBuild and a normal Make recipe invokes it.
Before CupidASM runs, CupidBuild rejects any unlisted `.elf` or `.exe` peer and
validates all five frozen tool images against the selected host profile. It
checks directory membership again after every attempted CupidASM and CupidDis
launch, including failure and timeout paths.
The source-head hosted link also builds CupidBuild beside CupidASM and the
other five production tools. This proves the command closure but does not add
CupidBuild to a checked seed.
The current checked Toolchain gate publishes 22 artifacts and passes all 22
assembler demos. It derives a separate six-tool candidate from the unchanged
five-tool seed. Its Linux proof freezes 58 sources and matches
22 C objects, startup, and all six final tool images between consecutive
generations. Both CupidBuild images assemble the checked startup into equal
relocatables and preserve prior output on a missing-source failure. Each
final-stage CupidDis also checks all six corresponding images for known code,
local targets, and code anchors. Both generations reject a private CupidBuild
copy whose entry instruction was replaced with an invalid opcode. Linux now
passes 23 failure, six help, and 29 success cases; Windows passes 12, six, and
16. The candidate is not a promoted seed.
>endtree

>h2 Overview
>tree open Overview
CupidASM is CupidOS's shared freestanding 16/32-bit x86 assembler. The same
parser, layout engine, instruction encoder, and artifact builder serve the
hosted command and the in-kernel JIT/AOT commands.

Source architecture:
- toolchain/cupidasm.cc and .h: language, layout, RAW, ELF32 relocatable, and
  fixed-image semantics
- toolchain/x86.cc: shared instruction encoding
- toolchain/elf32.cc: shared relocatable-object output
- kernel/lang/as.cc: 631 runtime definitions, VFS, fixed placement, and JIT/AOT
  execution and artifact-publication policy; the checked CupidC seed owns its
  normal object
- kernel/lang/as_elf.cc and .h: typed raw, relocatable, and executable request,
  raw-map rendering, rollback publication, and in-kernel CupidLD adapter
- toolchain/cupidasm_main.cc: hosted cupidasm command adapter

The normal image runs CupidASM from a checked five-tool seed. It owns five
production assembly transforms without first building a native hosted
command. The complete ownership audit assigns all 32 active assembly sources
to CupidASM, including five Toolchain startup inputs, and rejects ownerless
additions. ADR 0327 records that rule. The same checked trust unit supplies CupidObj for 192 transforms,
CupidLD for three root links, and CupidDis for six root inspections. Three
additional CupidDis inspections guard the user ELFs. Python verifies
and freezes all five images before each command. Linux uses the static i386
bootstrap seed. Windows uses the native PE32 execution seed for output-bearing
commands. Linux-contract work still crosses WSL. The native fixed-point driver
pairs that PE seed with a frozen Linux build plan. The
same runner executes checked production kernel,
generated-install, and user CupidC plus checked user CupidLD and CupidDis from caller-owned
frozen captures. It checks the complete live seed cohort again after each
command. Make passes wildcard-discovered output sources through $(sort ...)
before generation and link, so Windows and Linux use the same root order
regardless of host locale. ADR 0246 records this checked invocation boundary.

The Toolchain manifest includes a Cupid-built author stage. Python freezes the
publication facts, then frames a CUPMAN4 request with raw stage-three and
stage-four bytes for 62 pairs: 17 contract objects, 16 contract executables, 22
bootstrap C objects, one startup object, and six tool images. Stage-four
checked Linux CupidC compiles the C author, CupidASM supplies host startup, and
CupidLD links a static ELF on Linux or a validated native PE on Windows. The
PE uses the checked Windows runtime and exact imports. The author requires
regular, nonempty, byte-identical streams, hashes both sides, derives the 17 schema-v3 object
records, and checks executable pairs against artifact facts. It derives the
fixed-point summary from the exact pair inventories, so the request has no
caller all_equal field. Python repeats all 62 comparisons after author
acceptance and still owns filesystem capture, drift checks, rollback, and
atomic publication. It captures each side from a regular file through a
descriptor whose identity is checked before and after the read. CUPMAN2
remains the verifier.

A valid schema-v3 publication records exactly 22 artifacts, 75 publication
inputs, 58 bootstrap inputs, and 17 object comparisons, and CUPMAN2 must accept
it. Both checked Python contract launchers put this checkout ahead of installed
packages before importing `tools`. The direct contract passes 40 tests in
51.723 seconds, the publisher passes 65 in 7.258 seconds, and the pinned runner
passes 25 in 32.773 seconds. The host bootstrap log records the exact
publication identity and completion evidence.

The publication gate requires stage-three and stage-four identity, hosted
runtime checks, live-input checks, and native Windows verification. Normal
`all` uses poisoned-host Cupid tooling. Its exact artifact-size policy covers
fourteen paths: four OS artifacts, five Linux seed images, and five Windows
seed images. A CUPSIZE2 request lets the CupidC-built contract validate the
Windows manifest target, provenance, Linux parent link, exact five-tool
inventory, and observed sizes and digests. The focused semantic-contract,
checked-runner, and independent-policy modules contain 22, 16, and 13 tests,
for 51 total. They pass with four existing platform-specific skips. The host
bootstrap log records the complete source-consistent normal build, final
checked publication, and private four-vCPU proof for this increment. Exact OS
identities and runtime measurements stay there because this manual is an image
input.

CupidObj's 192 normal transforms include the disk-template, ISO, and guarded
profile-manifest operations. Checked CupidObj authors the pristine FAT16
prefix from private boot and kernel copies.
Python renders an independent oracle and requires byte parity. It then
extends fresh images, preserves the FAT partition when reusing a valid image,
stages frozen files, checks for live changes, and publishes the full image
atomically. ADR 0238 records this production handoff.
ADR 0244 records the guarded profile-manifest handoff.

The SMP trampoline is assembled and inspected in one guarded production
transaction. CupidASM emits a private 4,096-byte image and a source-derived
range map. Hostbuild requires that map to describe 0000h..001Eh as 16-bit
code, 001Fh..020Fh as data, 0210h..0253h as 32-bit code, and 0254h..0FFFh as
data. CupidDis reads the accepted map. Every instruction in the code ranges
must be known, and the four constant direct relative targets must land at
instruction starts in code of the same mode. A failed check leaves the
previous output unchanged. The private map is not installed in Cupid OS.

The SMP and bootloader paths now use one checked raw-image transaction. It owns
the output lock, frozen source and seed captures, drift checks, private
candidates, publication-boundary checks, and atomic replacement. Callers keep
their image and map policy. The SMP caller also checks the exact map bytes and
pins them through CupidDis. The expanded eleven-test suite passed in 1.708
seconds, including direct mismatch and live-output drift checks for both callers.
Parent-replacement tests exposed a POSIX candidate leak when private work lived
below the output parent. Private roots now live directly below the stable
repository root. Both caller modules pass all 10 tests on Windows and through
WSL, including parent replacement with no leaked candidate.

The ISR and context-switch objects use the same checked assembly transaction.
CupidASM writes each ELF32 relocatable to a private candidate. Hostbuild
validates its structure, requires executable section bytes, and asks CupidDis
to decode every executable byte. Only then may the object replace its previous
public version. Source, seed, candidate, output, or output-parent drift stops
publication. The final kernel gate checks both objects again as part of the
complete 431-input cohort. ADR 0286 records this object boundary.

The fifth transform builds test_iso/fixtures/big.bin from
test_iso/big_pattern.asm. Its concise `times 4096 db $` statement emits the
low byte of the current location, producing 00 through FF sixteen times.
Hostbuild freezes the source and seed, checks the exact 4,096-byte candidate,
and rechecks the live inputs before atomic publication. Python verifies the
bytes but does not author them. NASM freezes `$` across this TIMES statement,
so this fixture is the one production source exempt from optional NASM byte
parity. The other active sources keep their NASM comparisons.

Checked-seed CupidObj authors the enclosing ECMA-119 and Rock Ridge image
through `iso-fixture`, while CupidASM continues to own the 4,096-byte spanning
file. Python freezes the typed inventory, compares an independent render, and
publishes under a per-output lock. CupidASM remains at five production
transforms; the ISO handoff changes no assembler owner. ADR 0241 records the
image transfer.

Checked-seed CupidObj accepts:
  cupidobj ksyms-source SYMBOLS -o OUTPUT
It reads canonical CupidDis text and emits the exact packed kernel-symbol .cc
source. It keeps nonprivate local, global, and weak text symbols, sorts them by
address and input order, and keeps the first name at a shared address. Bad
rows, i386 address overflow, missing text, and resource exhaustion leave the
old output untouched. The normal kernel-symbol recipe now gives this command
CupidDis's exact text. Python freezes the inputs, checks the generated bytes
against an independent renderer, and publishes only a complete match.

The first direct Windows and Linux comparison matched 426 of 430 kernel
artifacts. The other four followed one JPEG object that host FFmpeg had
produced differently from the tracked progressive source. The repository
stores the accepted sequential baseline bytes. Hostbuild validates SOF0 or
SOF1 input, copies it exactly, rejects progressive, unsupported, or malformed
frames, and asks checked CupidObj to wrap the private snapshot. The normal
root no longer needs FFmpeg, jpegtran, djpeg, or cjpeg. The cross-host
artifact gate checks this byte-exact boundary after related build changes.

cupidasm.cc, x86.cc, and elf32.cc also belong to the checked fixed point.
Together with ctool.cc and cupiddis.cc, they complete the five shared
Toolchain roots. Native GCC or Clang rules remain only in explicit
native-oracle and hosted development targets. ADR 0126 records the naming
change and old-seed proof. ADR 0190 records the root command handoff.

The old kernel-only as_lex.c and as_parse.c implementations were removed.

Main command forms:
  as file.asm
  as -o output file.asm
  as -f bin --map output.map -o output.bin file.asm
  as -f elf32 -o output.o file.asm
  as -f exec -o output file.asm
  cupidasm file.asm
  cupidasm file.asm -o output
  cupidasm -o output file.asm

as is the JIT-first path. The older -o form writes a linked executable.
cupidasm writes a linked executable by default and derives its name when -o is
omitted. Both commands accept -f bin|elf32|exec. An explicit -f requires -o,
and raw output also requires --map. It writes the source-derived
cupid.raw-map.v2 origin, code16, code32, or data ranges, and ordered control
edges beside the flat bytes. ELF32 output remains an unlinked i386
ET_REL file with undefined symbols and relocations. It does not require main
or _start. Exec output selects main or _start and links with in-kernel CupidLD.

The raw image and map use one rollback publication. Both private candidates
are written before either target moves. A failed write or later replacement
restores the previous pair. If restoration fails, the backup stays available
for the next command to recover. Matching v1 completion records are written
only after both public targets move. A later command can use a valid record to
finish private cleanup; without one, retained backups are restored. This VFS
path does not write a pending record or absence tombstone before mutation, and
its recovery removes a readable target before replacing it from backup.
This is command-level
rollback, not a crash-atomic two-file VFS transaction or a concurrent-writer
lock. Native Windows fixed-point CupidASM links the publication startup and
runtime objects with their exact Kernel32 imports. Its behavior relink is
validated against the same plan-derived import profile. Linux native Windows
evidence reconstructs CupidASM with the same closure. ADR 0337 records the
boundary.

Source-backed limits:
- code buffer: 1 MB
- data buffer: 1 MB
- each source file: 4 MB
- private job arena: 8 MB
- runtime definitions: at most 8192; the checked kernel catalogue has 631
- nested %include depth: 32
- mapped AOT executable span: at most 2 MB
- JIT/AOT code base: 0x01A00000
- JIT/AOT data base: 0x01B00000
>endtree

>h2 Entry Points and Calling Convention
>tree open Entry Points and Calling Convention
Accepted entry labels:
- main:
- _start:

Calling convention:
- cdecl
- caller cleans explicit arguments after calls

RET accepts an optional unsigned 16-bit byte count. RET 4 pops the return
address and then advances ESP by four bytes. The hosted CupidC
structure-return ABI uses this form to remove its hidden result pointer. The
caller still cleans ordinary explicit cdecl arguments.

CupidASM encodes RET 4 as C2 04 00 and rejects RET 65536 because the operand
does not fit the 16-bit field. CupidDis renders C2 04 00 as ret 0x4.

Hosted CupidDis can inspect one flat image that mixes 16-bit code, 32-bit code,
and literal data. Start with --mode 16 or --mode 32 and add each later range:
  cupiddis --raw --mode 16 --range-at 0x1f:data --range-at 0x210:32 \
      --range-at 0x254:data --base 0x8000 kernel/smp_trampoline.bin

Range offsets are relative to the start of the file. They must increase and
remain inside the input. CupidDis decodes code ranges and prints data ranges
as DB rows without decoding them. The caller places each code transition
between instructions. The older --mode-at OFFSET:16|32 spelling remains
available for code-only maps.

CupidASM can write the same information from its parsed source:
  cupidasm -f bin --map boot.map -o boot.bin boot.asm
  cupiddis --require-known --raw --range-map boot.map boot.bin

The cupid.raw-map.v2 file records the image size, ORG base, coalesced range
starts, and ordered source-resolved control edges. Instructions use their active
BITS mode. Data declarations, alignment,
and reserved storage are data. CupidDis rejects stale sizes, missing or repeated
fields, invalid kinds, and unordered starts before decoding.

Checked-seed CupidASM writes cupid.raw-map.v2. It keeps the range records and
adds an exact list of source-resolved calls and jumps. Each row records the
instruction offset, relative, far, or indirect kind, local, external, or
unprovable class, resolved destination, target mode, and far segment when one
is encoded. CupidDis still reads v1. Strict validation can require v2:
  cupiddis --require-known --require-local-targets \
      --require-source-edges --raw --range-map boot.map boot.bin

The added rule rejects a branch that reaches the wrong valid instruction
start and checks immediate far mode transitions. Indirect register or memory
targets remain explicitly unprovable because runtime state supplies their
destination. The guarded boot and SMP transactions require this rule and keep
the earlier image on failure. ADR 0340 records the source contract, and ADR
0336 records seed carriage and adoption.

The normal boot edge uses the shared map transaction. Hostbuild freezes the
production manifest, five checked tools, source, and output boundary. CupidASM
writes private image and map candidates. CupidDis requires complete known code
coverage, validates nine constant direct relative targets, and binds every v2
edge to its source-resolved destination before hostbuild atomically publishes
the exact 2,560-byte image. Failures preserve the previous image and leave no
public map. ADR 0283 records the Make graph cutover.

Checked-seed CupidDis can validate one or more files without printing a
listing:
  cupiddis --require-known FILE [FILE...]

Success means that every selected code region has zero unknown, invalid, and
truncated instructions. A failure names its input and reports all four counts,
including known instructions. CupidDis keeps checking later inputs. Declared
raw data and non-executable ELF regions do not count. Ordinary rendering still
accepts one file and keeps its existing output. The normal kernel path runs
strict validation and flat extraction against one frozen cohort of all 429
audited root object outputs plus the pass-one and final kernel ELFs. Its
9,076-byte graph-ordered manifest lists those 431 unique paths and has SHA-256
4f1936423ae06418fc2f75603c29a91997608fe82f48c323321523aed25a2ab0.
An immutable first-opcode index preserves exhaustive selection, and the
checked 128 KiB throughput contract passes within 30 seconds. ADR 0262 records
the command, ADR 0266 records indexed decoding, and ADR 0265 records
checked-seed carriage and production adoption.

The promoted checked CupidDis seeds provide the raw local target policy:
  cupiddis --require-known --require-local-targets --raw \
      --range-map boot.map boot.bin

The option requires --require-known. This production call also supplies --raw.
CupidDis decodes the image in two passes, records every instruction start, and
validates constant direct CALL, short and near JMP, and short and near
conditional branches. A target must stay in the image and land at an
instruction start in code with the same mode. Failures are counted as outside
the image, in data, in the wrong code mode, or in the middle of an
instruction. Raw 16-bit address mapping may wrap at 64 KiB; input larger than
65,536 bytes is rejected as ambiguous.

The bootloader supplies nine direct targets and the SMP trampoline supplies
four. Production hostbuild now passes --require-local-targets for both guarded
raw images. Five active-source tests cover all nine bootloader targets and all
four trampoline targets. The two cross-platform seed carriage fixtures are
narrower: promoted Linux and native Windows CupidDis each accept EB 00 C3 and
reject EB 7F, with one direct target in each fixture. Ten hostbuild tests also
pass. The normal bootloader and SMP transactions retain this enforcement. Far
pointers and indirect transfers remain outside the policy. A bad
displacement that reaches another valid same-mode instruction start cannot be
distinguished without label metadata. ADR 0300 records the source rule.

CupidDis also accepts the option for static ELF32 relocatable
objects:
  cupiddis --require-known --require-local-targets program.o

Each executable PROGBITS section gets one reusable instruction-start map for
rendering, relocation ownership, and anchor checks. Local-target validation
adds only its required target walk. An unrelocated direct relative target must stay in that section
and land on an instruction start there. A relocation at the operand field
leaves the destination for link time. The existing relocation-ownership rule
still checks the field. Failures distinguish a target outside the section from
one in the middle of an instruction.

The active ISR and context-switch objects pass this checked-seed policy. Eleven
ISR call relocations stay outside the local count. Changing one context-switch
branch byte produces one mid-instruction failure. The promoted seeds carry this
form, and production object publication selects it before replacement. ADR
0309 records the source boundary, and ADR 0312 records carriage and adoption.

Checked CupidDis also accepts the option for linked i386 ELF32 images. It
scans nonoverlapping file-backed executable load regions twice and accepts a
cross-region target only when it lands on an instruction start. The linked
report separates targets outside loaded memory, in loaded memory without
file-backed executable code, and inside an instruction. A PT_DYNAMIC or
PT_INTERP header rejects the image as outside the static certification domain.
Typed and CLI negative cases cover both forms. The normal kernel transaction
applies the linked rule to its frozen pass-one and final ELFs before CupidObj
flattening. ADR 0314 records the decoder boundary, and ADR 0318 records seed
carriage and production adoption.

CupidASM can type an exported or imported function explicitly:
  global dispatch:function
  extern scheduler_resume:function

These declarations write STT_FUNC symbols. An unannotated symbol remains
STT_NOTYPE, so exported data is not treated as code. A missing or unsupported
type reports CT6000018 without publishing a partial object.

Checked-seed CupidDis validates code anchors in static ELF32 objects:
  cupiddis --require-known --require-code-anchors program.o
  cupiddis --require-known --require-code-anchors program.elf

For ET_REL input, every defined STT_FUNC must begin at a decoded instruction
in executable PROGBITS. Undefined functions and other symbol types are
excluded. For ET_EXEC input, the rule also counts e_entry and requires every
anchor to begin in file-backed executable code. Function aliases count
separately. Failures distinguish anchors outside code from anchors in the
middle of an instruction. ADR 0320 records the linked source boundary, ADR
0323 records its linked promotion and adoption, ADR 0335 records the
relocatable form, and ADR 0336 records its promotion and active adoption. The
ISR, context-switch, and hosted startup objects publish 68 typed functions.
All fourteen CupidBuild Windows startup exports now use the function annotation,
and the active-source contract rejects an untyped startup export.

Source-head and checked-seed CupidDis also check relocations that target executable sections
in ELF32 relocatable objects. R_386_PC32 must begin at a decoded four-byte
relative field. R_386_32 must begin at a decoded four-byte non-relative field.
The typed report counts total and unmatched executable relocations, and
--require-known rejects a nonzero unmatched count even when every byte decodes.
Data-section relocations do not enter this code check. The checked Linux and
Windows seeds carry the rule, so guarded CupidASM objects must pass it before
publication. ADR 0290 records the relocation boundary, and ADR 0292 records
the seed promotion.

An earlier poisoned-host normal `make -j2` passed in 1,057.969 seconds. The
separate strict gate ran before CupidObj flattened the kernel. It produced a 9,039,936-byte
pass-one ELF with SHA-256
b21fa8954499a7857ee4b12fa3950fcc08ff3c6a6234c8ae72effc38c51fdc6d, a
9,162,816-byte final ELF with SHA-256
a0b57cd886369762b65d657bb3f2915ada8f30b52102535add89466eaf4f5976, and an
8,946,332-byte raw kernel with SHA-256
4f5f2591d01bcc4007773844e9bfb8112a16dd17fbd178014cc2056fefaab67d.
At that checkpoint, the hostbuild transaction froze the selected seed manifest
and all five artifacts, the 431-entry input manifest and cohort, and the existing
kernel.bin boundary. Checked CupidDis validates the private cohort before
checked CupidObj flattens the frozen final ELF. Hostbuild rechecks live trust
inputs and the output before parent-relative atomic publication. Every failure
preserved the prior raw kernel. The transaction passed with exit 0 in 187.054
seconds and published the same 8,946,332-byte raw kernel and SHA-256. The
hostbuild suites each passed 31 tests on Windows and in WSL, with
platform-specific skips. Moving private flatten extraction onto the shared
pinned-path helper remains deferred maintenance.

The former clean forced build finished in 653.2 seconds. Its 2,560-byte boot
image has SHA-256
46cc9778da2b5cc5e8f04d7cc4b07243c3e07d466626ad84fb813dc6fef3a0d3.
The preceding clean build's 9,190,860-byte pass-one ELF has SHA-256
aee9e505c92a1e701bea05897e0cb1901a13b3d5eacecf2512607a608e0e3efd,
the 9,313,740-byte final ELF has SHA-256
5b179b938edb74c3edec59c2cc223366b5bb521c2a88f9a12f970a2b8b2bbaa1,
and the 9,096,008-byte raw kernel has SHA-256
9222973f7000f3e95dfd786ad2cd22ad4d90f5cc1e08a47537fe6c71603c7f51.

That checkpoint predates the guarded normal bootloader edge and this CTXT
record. The live policy records exact kernel sizes in the host bootstrap log.
Embedding those self-referential values here would change the kernel they
identify.
The published 209,715,200-byte image has SHA-256
dfdda396c6995458bd4e6185ec7e36645ebf3c193cd303ce495135c5d015b59e.
A private four-vCPU /bin/ls.cc JIT boot passed from that image in 46.9 seconds.
Definitive four-CPU E1000 and RTL8139 boot frontiers passed from that image
with exits 0 in 794.034 and 758.667 seconds. Both passed SMP, frontier,
framebuffer, AC97, and PC speaker checks without changing the source image.

The production boot source assembles to an exact 2,560-byte image with
SHA-256 46cc9778da2b5cc5e8f04d7cc4b07243c3e07d466626ad84fb813dc6fef3a0d3.
CupidASM and the optional NASM oracle produce the same bytes. This image sets
the stack top to 0x01100000, reads through LBA 20479, and declares FAT16 at
LBA 20480.

The hosted i386 contract compiles the unchanged CupidC, CupidASM, CupidDis,
CupidLD, and CupidObj command closures with CupidC under the checked four-byte
ABI.
CupidASM assembles the repository _start and int 0x80 system-call boundary.
CupidLD links five deterministic static i386 commands without unresolved
symbols.

The repository runtime provides the checked file, heap, memory, string, errno,
getcwd, and formatted-output calls used by those commands. It has unbuffered
streams and single-threaded process state. Each closure object is emitted twice
before the repeated links. A sixth executable checks allocation, tail release,
files, seeks, errors, arguments, memory comparison, and strings. WSL checks raw
and ELF32 assembly, include resolution, raw disassembly with mode changes,
fixed-address linking, object wrapping, and missing files against the native
tools. Successful output matches byte for byte or as text. Invalid assembly
and malformed linker input follow the same failure behavior and diagnostics.

The five static tools now cross one complete stage boundary under WSL.
Generation-one CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD build all 19 C objects, startup,
and the five stage-two images. Stage-two CupidC, CupidASM, and CupidLD repeat
the build for stage three. Every object and linked CupidC, CupidASM, CupidDis,
CupidLD, and CupidObj image matches byte for byte. Both stages also agree on
raw and ELF32 assembly, disassembly, symbol listing, fixed-address and scripted
linking, binary and canonical-text wrapping, executable flattening, help, and
useful failures.

Checked-seed CupidLD also links ordered static i386 objects into one fixed-layout
PE32 console image. Image base is 0x00400000, and text begins at RVA 0x1000.
Nonempty read-only, writable, and BSS sections follow in that order at the next
page boundary. Empty output categories do not get PE section headers. The
image reserves and commits a one MiB stack. Its heap reserves one MiB and
commits 4 KiB. It has no base relocations. Repeatable import options add canonical
descriptors, lookup tables, IAT cells, and strings in writable .idata.
CupidLD orders imports with an in-place heap, rejects repeated slots without a
second scan over earlier requests, and keeps name imports below the PE32
high-bit boundary. Writable executable input is rejected.

The Windows entry uses `call dword [__imp_Name]`. CupidASM emits FF 15 with a
known, zero-addend R_386_32 relocation. CupidLD binds it to the IAT cell. A
direct call emits R_386_PC32 and fails before publication. Both rebuilt
assemblers produce the same Windows startup object. Both CupidC stages compile
the same freestanding main, and both CupidLD stages produce the same PE bytes.
Windows validates and runs the image, checks its exact stdout and empty stderr,
and requires exit 37. The validator checks the fixed stack and heap fields,
reconstructs the exact .idata layout, and
the report retains both object and image pairs plus the observed Windows
result. The promoted Linux seed carries the 5/22/21 behavior matrix and this
command.

Checked-seed CupidLD uses an adjacent candidate created with exclusive-create
semantics for both ELF and PE output. It writes and closes the candidate, then
reopens the file and checks its size and contents before one replacement call.
Injected write, close, verification, and replacement errors preserve an
existing destination. Cleanup is attempted but not guaranteed. On POSIX,
CupidLD requests mode 0777; the process umask may remove any permission bits.
The directory must remain stable under the caller's control, and the CLI does
not lock or pin the output path.

At the pre-stack-probe checkpoint, the checked i386 Linux seed included
CupidASM. Its manifest bound the exact assembler image to the target ABI,
source revision, producer lineage, source plan, and link order. The bootstrap
froze the verified manifest and binaries and copied its 50-input source closure
into a private root. The closure included link.ld, the small Windows probe, the
native Windows tool runtime and startup, CupidLD publication runtime and
bridge, the direct contract, and hosted declarations. Checked CupidASM
assembled stage-two startup there, then the stage-two assembler produced
byte-identical stage-three startup below the same root. The harness checked the
private and live closures at each stage and behavior boundary. The complete
seed build also compared all 19 C objects and five linked tools and ran the
5/18/16 behavior matrix. All evidence was published together only after the
gate passed.
An independent poisoned-host reproof passed in 766.9 seconds. All five seed
images matched stage two, and stage two matched stage three across the same
19 C objects, startup, five tools, and behavior matrix.

At the checked Windows execution-seed checkpoint, source head assembled the
native Windows tool entry and twelve shared cdecl API bridges through stages
two and three, plus
four publication bridges for CupidLD. Those startup objects matched. CupidLD linked all five native
tool closures with their matching runtime objects. Windows runs help plus
useful success and failure paths for every tool and checks the direct runtime
contract. CupidDis checks quoted raw-input parity with the Linux tool. CupidLD
checks exact output, candidate collision, replacement failure, and cleanup.
Those images formed the preceding checked Windows execution seed for output-bearing
production work. Source head freezes that seed and the verified Linux plan
separately. The seed builds stage two, stage two builds stage three, and stage
three builds stage four. Stages two and three are transition generations. The
convergence check compares stages three and four, then runs the behavior
matrix. The old stage-two to stage-three comparison stopped safely at
cupidobj_main after 821.9 seconds on Windows and 883.3 seconds on Linux. New
stack-probe code generation changed compiler-produced objects. Later uncapped
proofs passed the final-pair artifact and behavior gates on the same frozen
50-input snapshot. Windows finished in 20 minutes 43 seconds, and Linux
finished in 24 minutes 22 seconds. Both runs began from uncommitted source and
remain preliminary history. Linux later passed a clean proof from revision
5d690c7508cc031a0cb32b2963bf16300b32e267 in 1,383.775 seconds. Stages three
and four matched 19 C objects, startup, and five tools, and both passed 5/18/16
behavior. The promoted stage-four CupidASM is 454,160 bytes with SHA-256
7d6c4a538dcbb04663514445474dae394a6d8fead08c454885315777bf3e3867.
The 5,573-byte seed manifest has SHA-256
f8528f5fcb68473f5078427dfc1c7dd5fce78413a56b45c6aa831971d827ca4f
and binds generation four, the clean revision, the 50-input snapshot, and the
stage-three producers. A 1,411.998-second reproof reproduced all five promoted
seed images and the same fixed point. Native Windows then passed a clean proof
from revision bd8fd28e6e0e097c4ee3a5c5de0b0706b7153930 in 1,152.7 seconds.
Stages three and four matched 20 C objects, two assembly objects, and five
tools, and both passed 5/5/5 behavior. The old seed matched stage two for
CupidLD and CupidObj but not for CupidASM, CupidC, or CupidDis. The promoted
CupidASM image is 437,760 bytes with SHA-256
8134a9400c4cae7e6c7e72989aa9b23bbdcb56ba4d52a9ebb15363128e4a1f18.
The 2,118-byte Windows manifest has SHA-256
96bb80521ba679161008c9fa0891aff9d7ae172868cde107ff1a78feebdccfc9.
A 1,130.9-second reproof matched all five initial seed images and repeated the
same fixed point. ADR 0280 records the Linux promotion, and ADR 0281 records
the Windows promotion.

A preliminary Linux behavior reconstruction found that cupiddis_main.cc lacked
_WIN32=1. Four native tools already matched. The corrected Windows profile,
CupidDis parity test, and audit guard now cover all five driver mains.

make verify-bootstrap-seed validates the files without executing them.
make bootstrap-from-seed performs the Linux staged build. The public
bootstrap-windows command performs the native PE build from the Windows
execution seed and Linux plan seed. Operators run make
verify-windows-bootstrap-seed, then make bootstrap-windows-from-seed. The
second target publishes only under build/bootstrap/checked-windows-seed. Its
dry run and two Make contract tests pass. Windows still runs the complete
Linux Toolchain contract cohort through WSL. Output-bearing production recipes
and the user ABI contract run the checked native PE32 execution seed directly.
Normal Cupid OS C roots and Toolchain contracts use checked CupidC. A host
compiler remains only for explicit native oracles and hosted development
commands.

>box
If an asm demo crashes strangely, stack cleanup is the first thing to inspect.
Forgetting add esp, N*4 after a call with pushed arguments is the most common
cause.
>endbox
>endtree

>h2 Syntax
>tree open Syntax
Source-backed syntax from the shared assembler:
- Intel-style mnemonics
- case-insensitive registers, mnemonics, directives, labels, and kernel
  runtime definitions in the in-OS profile
- ; line comments
- decimal, hex, and binary or binary-suffix integers
- labels with name:, dot-local labels, and data labels without a colon
- equ constants, %define constants, $, $$, and checked arithmetic expressions
- .text, .data, .bss
- BITS 16/32 and ORG
- db, dw, dd, dq, strings, escapes, and times
- resb, resw, resd
- rb, rw, rd
- reserve alias
- align POWER_OF_TWO[, FILL_BYTE]
- global
- extern
- %include "path.asm" with cycle, missing-file, and depth diagnostics

Raw output has one flat address space. Its source accepts one ORG and one
section identity. An equ definition is sectionless, so it may appear before an
explicit section directive. Without one, the first section-bound statement
claims implicit .text; labels count because they name a section position.
Repeating the selected section is valid. A second ORG or a different section
reports an exact source diagnostic and writes no output. ELF32 and fixed-image
requests retain their independent section layouts.

align advances to the next requested boundary. The boundary must be a
nonzero power of two. The optional byte fill defaults to zero. Raw output
uses the absolute ORG address, ELF32 output records the section alignment,
and fixed images include the absolute region base. NOBITS padding changes
memory size without writing file bytes and therefore accepts only zero fill.

Relative includes resolve in this order:
1) the including source's parent directory
2) the shell's current working directory
3) the VFS root /

The CWD entry is omitted when it is already /. This supports helpers next to
their source, shared files selected from the current directory, and absolute
repository paths without making the assembler depend on ambient host paths.
>endtree

>h2 Instruction Coverage
>tree open Instruction Coverage
CupidASM implements the cleanly expressible NASM-compatible surface required
by active CupidOS sources; it does not claim unrestricted NASM compatibility.

The checked seed and source head have 604 x86 rows, 249 canonical mnemonics,
64 register names, and fingerprint 55A8970F. Every encodable row has one
deterministic witness in each legal mode. The 1,202 witnesses pass the real
encoder, both real decoders, and exact-form replay. The contract also checks
all aliases, invalid rows, illegal modes, row flags, and 2,641 proper byte
prefixes under digest 8C570035. A source-level selector test assembles through
CupidASM and requires CupidDis to recognize the exact bytes and render stable
canonical aliases. This is exhaustive by form and legal mode; focused
contracts cover additional operand and value combinations.

The catalogue includes signed x87
FILD and FISTP memory operands at 16, 32, and 64 bits and canonical SETP and
SETNP byte predicates in 16-bit and 32-bit modes. FILD and FISTP register, byte-memory,
and 80-bit memory forms are rejected without output. The seed's four SHRD rows cover 16-bit and 32-bit register
or memory destinations with either an immediate byte or fixed CL count. Both
modes honor operand-size and address-size overrides. Active checked-CupidC
objects decode `shrd eax, edi, cl` directly. The latest preceding row encodes
FSUB ST(1), ST(0) as DC E9. The four earlier x87 rows cover 80-bit
FLD and FSTP memory operands, i686 FUCOMIP ST0, ST(i), and operand-free FLDZ
for automatic long double values and truth tests. ADR 0208 records the seed
promotion. Active-source contracts
cover every instruction in all 22 demos and the four boot/kernel assembly
sources. Common families include:
- mov, lea, xchg
- push, pop, call, ret, and ret imm16 such as RET 4
- jmp and common conditional jumps
- add, sub, cmp, test
- and, or, xor, not, neg
- mul, imul, div, idiv
- shifts and rotates
- nop, including ordinary 66 90 and 0F 1F /0 compiler padding
- five exact decode-only Clang padding NOPs with two through six leading 66
  bytes; CupidASM cannot emit their redundant prefixes
- hlt, cli, sti, iret, leave
- jcc aliases including parity aliases: jp/jpe and jnp/jpo
- all sixteen i686 conditional moves with 16-bit or 32-bit register and memory
  sources, plus conventional aliases
- SETP and SETNP with a byte register or memory destination; SETPE and SETPO
  are not aliases in this source-driven slice
- GUI-mode `dis` keeps its listing in the terminal and mirrors it to serial
  after the shell's normal sink and redirection checks
- three-operand IMUL with a 16-bit or 32-bit register destination, a
  same-width register or memory source, and either a full or signed-byte
  immediate
- privileged/system opcodes: lgdt/lidt/sgdt/sidt, invlpg, ltr/str/sldt,
  smsw/lmsw, cpuid, rdtsc, rdmsr/wrmsr, wbinvd/invd, clts,
  sysenter/sysexit/syscall, iretd/retf
- atomic helpers: bswap, xadd, cmpxchg, lock-prefixed memory operations
- sized memory inc/dec and reserve directives

The promoted i386 Linux seed and source head carry all 604 shared x86 rows in
CupidASM and CupidDis, including FILD, FISTP, SETP, and SETNP. The catalogue also includes the IMUL and
padding NOP families, FLDZ, forward x87 stack subtraction, and SHRD. ADR 0208
records the x87 form's seed carriage, and ADR 0228 records the first SHRD seed
promotion. ADR 0243
records the preceding seed, ADR 0252 records the x87 integer forms, ADR 0258
records the preceding seed, and ADR 0259 records the parity predicates. ADRs
0265, 0280, 0292, 0312, and 0318 record preceding promotions. ADR 0323 records
the current promotion.

Floating-point and SIMD coverage includes:
- FPU state: FXSAVE, FXRSTOR, FINIT, FNINIT, FWAIT, LDMXCSR, STMXCSR
- SSE scalar (23): MOVSS/SD, ADDSS/SD, and more
- SSE packed (24): MOVAPS/UPS, ADDPS/PD, SHUFPS, CMPPS, and more
- x87 (29 in the checked seed): FILD, FISTP, FLD, FLDZ, FUCOMIP, FSIN, FPATAN,
  F2XM1, FYL2X, and more
- Register tokens: XMM0-7 and ST0-7

FSUB ST1, ST0 emits DC E9 and stores ST1 - ST0 in ST1. ST0 is the fixed
second operand. The older FSUBR ST1, ST0 form remains DC E1.

Use the shipped demos under /demos as the compatibility baseline.
Coverage demos include include_helper.asm, jcc_aliases.asm,
reserve_directives.asm, parity_priv.asm, and parity_gfx2d.asm.
>endtree

>h2 JIT Mode
>tree open JIT Mode
as file.asm reads the source through VFS, asks shared CupidASM for a fixed
code/data image, zeroes each full memory region including BSS, and runs main
or _start immediately.

JIT mode registers 631 case-insensitive constants and absolute kernel symbols
so asm programs can call into the OS directly. Examples from kernel/lang/as.cc:
- print, println (console output)
- vfs_open, vfs_read, vfs_write, vfs_close, vfs_seek, vfs_stat, vfs_readdir
- vfs_mkdir, vfs_unlink, vfs_rename, vfs_copy_file, vfs_read_all, vfs_write_all
- date/time helpers
- mouse and keyboard helpers
- graphics helpers
- panic/debug helpers

Direct gfx2d drawing uses process-wide render state. Pair
gfx2d_fullscreen_enter with gfx2d_fullscreen_exit, or use the retained
gui_win_begin_paint/gui_win_end_paint scope. The compatibility
gui_win_draw_frame call must end with gui_win_flip. Process cleanup releases
an abandoned scope before the PID is reused. The parity_gfx2d demo acquires
fullscreen ownership before initializing and using its offscreen surface.

The shell snapshots the explicit CupidASM code and data regions before a
nested program starts and restores them afterward. CupidC and CupidASM may use
different fixed regions without corrupting one another.

During a DEBUG boot, the kernel self-test assembles
/demos/include_feature.asm through the same VFS path and checks all 631
definitions before the desktop starts.

>button ls /demos | shell:ls /demos
>button cat /demos/hello.asm | shell:cat /demos/hello.asm
>button Run Hello Demo | shell:as /demos/hello.asm
>endtree

>h2 Artifact Output
>tree open Artifact Output
The public kernel adapter asks shared CupidASM for raw bytes or an ELF32
relocatable object. Exec output applies the caller-priority MAIN and _START
entry names, publishes the selected spelling, and promotes only that code
label. In-kernel CupidLD resolves the object and writes the executable at the
existing 0x01A00000 text address. JIT continues to use a fixed image directly.

Common patterns:
  as -o /home/hello /demos/hello.asm
  as -f bin --map /home/mixed.map -o /home/mixed.bin /home/mixed.asm
  as -f elf32 -o /home/module.o /home/module.asm
  as -f exec -o /home/hello /demos/hello.asm
  cupidasm /demos/hello.asm -o /home/hello
  cupidasm /demos/hello.asm

If cupidasm is called without -o, the output name is derived from the
source path by stripping .asm.

BIN output requires both -o and --map. The map records the raw origin and
source-derived CODE16, CODE32, and DATA ranges. ELF32 keeps an unlinked ET_REL
object with undefined symbols and relocations, so it does not need an entry
label. EXEC retains the existing entry selection and CupidLD link. Both
historical -o orders continue to mean EXEC.

The publisher writes both raw candidates before moving either target. A write
or later replacement failure restores the previous image and map. This is a
running-command rollback boundary. Matching v1 completion records are written
only after both public targets move. A later command can use a valid record to
finish private cleanup; without one, retained backups are restored. The VFS
publisher has no pending record or absence tombstone before mutation, and its
recovery removes a readable target before replacing it from backup. This is
not a crash-atomic two-file transaction or a concurrent-writer lock. The
hosted command uses a separate linked v2/v3 recovery protocol.

The adapter accepts code-only and code/data/BSS objects and leaves its output
empty when assembly, entry selection, linking, or validation fails. The loader
accepts the historical adjacent 1 MB code and data placement as one bounded
2 MB mapped span.

This fixed-address compatibility format supports one loaded assembly image at
a time. CupidLD and the final loader own distinct concurrent process
placement; the assembler language does not.

A private guest smoke ran `as -o /hello-aot /demos/hello.asm`, followed by
`exec /hello-aot`. It passed in 79.661 seconds. CupidASM produced a 15,680-byte
ET_REL object, and in-kernel CupidLD linked an 8,536-byte ELF at 0x01A00000
with two PT_LOAD segments. PID 4 exited cleanly.
ADR 0337 records the raw, relocatable, executable, and publication boundary.
>endtree

>h2 Disassembly Workflow
>tree open Disassembly Workflow
Useful inspection commands:
- exec -d /home/hello
- dis file.elf
- dis file.cc
- cc -d file.cc

CupidASM therefore uses the same source and binary inspection commands as
CupidC.
>endtree

>h2 Demo Sources
>tree open Demo Sources
The source tree and boot image ship 22 asm demos under /demos:
- asm_compat_reserve.asm
- bubblesort.asm
- data.asm
- factorial.asm
- fibonacci.asm
- fpu_kernel.asm
- fs_syscalls.asm
- hello.asm
- include_feature.asm
- include_helper.asm
- jcc_aliases.asm
- loop.asm
- math.asm
- parity_core.asm
- parity_diag.asm
- parity_gfx2d.asm
- parity_priv.asm
- reserve_directives.asm
- simd_blur.asm
- stack.asm
- syscall_table_demo.asm
- syscall_vfs_extended_demo.asm

The hosted corpus check assembles every demo twice as a fixed image with
implicit externs disabled. Its binding spellings match the kernel adapter.
parity_gfx2d.asm receives both gfx2d_fullscreen_enter and
gfx2d_fullscreen_exit, so every fullscreen cleanup path resolves.

For persistent copies, make sync-demos refreshes /home/demos in cupidos.img.

>button ls /demos | shell:ls /demos
>button cat /demos/factorial.asm | shell:cat /demos/factorial.asm
>button cat /demos/loop.asm | shell:cat /demos/loop.asm
>endtree

>h2 Practical Workflow
>tree open Practical Workflow
Workflow:
1) inspect a shipped demo under /demos
2) run it with as
3) copy or create your own version under /home
4) assemble to ELF with as -o or cupidasm
5) run the result with exec

This keeps asm aligned with the same source-first workflow as the rest of
CupidOS.
>endtree

>h2 Current checked assembler and disassembler
>tree
Both assembler seeds keep absolute EQU definitions outside raw section
claims. Both disassembler seeds require every executable ELF relocation to
begin at a compatible decoded four-byte field. The guarded ISR and context
switch objects must pass that rule before publication. They also carry the
raw local-target policy used by the production bootloader and SMP trampoline
transactions.

The Linux and Windows five-tool cohorts pass their fixed-point comparison and
behavior gates. This establishes seed carriage for the shared assembler and
disassembler rules, including section-local targets in static relocatable
objects. The guarded ISR and context-switch transactions select that object
rule after structural ELF validation. Relocated external operands remain under
the relocation-ownership check and are excluded from the local-target count.
Far pointers and indirect transfers remain outside the local-target count.
Linked ELF target proof is part of the current checked-seed policy. The normal
kernel transaction runs it over the frozen pass-one and final ELFs after the
broad production scan and before CupidObj flattening. Any failure keeps the
previous raw kernel. The current publication and CUPMAN2 evidence are recorded
in the overview above.
Exact seed identities remain in the host bootstrap log.

Both checked seeds carry the static ELF code-anchor rule for entry and defined
function symbols. The normal kernel publisher applies it to the pass-one and
final ELFs before CupidObj flattening. Minimal DWARF source information remains
separate work.

The in-OS raw adapter accepts fixed 16-bit code, fixed 32-bit code, or a
borrowed map of code16, code32, and data ranges. Its strict option checks the
shared CupidDis summary and returns an error without a listing when selected
code is unknown, invalid, or truncated. Existing CupidC JIT listings still use
the permissive fixed-32 wrapper. The DEBUG self-test covers one mixed map and
one strict rejection through the public adapter.
>endtree

